<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-health/foundational/profession-and-science-of-public-health">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1920 C.-E. A. Winslow defined public health as <strong>"the science and art of
    preventing disease, prolonging life and promoting health through the organised efforts
    of society"</strong> — and every word earns its place. Not the doctor's art of curing
    one patient, but the organised effort of whole societies. That is the first foundational
    knowledge area of every CEPH-accredited programme: <em>explain public health history,
    philosophy, and values; identify the core functions of public health and the 10
    Essential Services</em>. The philosophical shift is from the individual case to the
    population: a physician asks what is wrong with this patient; a public-health
    professional asks why this population is sick and what would make it healthier.
  </p>

  <h2>A profession built by counting</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="from one doctor's map to global eradication — the milestones that built the profession" widget="timeline" config={phTimeline} />
  <p>
    The pattern in the timeline is worth naming, because it is the philosophy: public health
    advances when <em>evidence</em> precedes action. Jenner and Snow acted on observation;
    WHO institutionalised cooperation; smallpox proved the model end to end. CEPH's
    foundational objective 6 — explain the critical importance of evidence in advancing
    public health knowledge — is not an abstract value; it is literally how the profession
    was assembled.
  </p>

  <h2>Core functions and the 10 Essential Services</h2>
  <p>
    The 1988 Institute of Medicine report <em>The Future of Public Health</em> fixed three
    <strong>core functions</strong>: <em>assessment</em> (monitor and measure what a
    population's health actually is), <em>policy development</em> (turn evidence into
    decisions), and <em>assurance</em> (guarantee the services get delivered). The US
    <strong>10 Essential Public Health Services</strong> (updated 2020) translate those
    functions into an operating checklist:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>assess and monitor population health; investigate, diagnose and address health hazards and root causes</li>
    <li>communicate effectively to inform and educate; strengthen, support and empower communities</li>
    <li>create, champion and implement policies, plans and laws; enforce laws and regulations that protect health</li>
    <li>assure equitable access to care; build a strong, diverse, skilled public-health workforce and infrastructure</li>
    <li>improve and innovate through ongoing evaluation, research and continuous quality improvement</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notice what the list is <em>not</em>: it is not a hospital system. Clinical care for
    individuals appears once, inside "assure access" — the rest is population work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The instrument kit that makes this work is CEPH's objective 3: quantitative and
    qualitative methods for describing and assessing a population's health. Epidemiology
    and biostatistics count who gets sick, where, and of what — the surveillance that made
    Snow's map possible and that cornered smallpox — while qualitative methods capture the
    beliefs and circumstances behind the numbers. Objective 4 adds the discipline's
    honesty clause: morbidity and mortality are always broken down by population, because
    averages hide disparities. A city's life expectancy is a different number in its
    richest and poorest wards, and acting on <em>that</em> difference is what the
    profession's values — equity among them — actually commit it to.
  </p>

  <h2>The prevention pyramid</h2>
  <p>
    CEPH objective 5 asks you to "discuss the science of primary, secondary and tertiary
    prevention". Public health arranges intervention in three tiers:
    <strong>primary prevention</strong> stops disease before it starts (vaccination, clean
    water, seatbelts, clean-air laws); <strong>secondary prevention</strong> catches disease
    early, before symptoms or damage (screening for cervical cancer, blood-pressure checks);
    <strong>tertiary prevention</strong> limits the damage once disease exists
    (rehabilitation, complication management). The pyramid's logic is economic as well as
    moral: the higher up you act, the more people you touch per pound — and the less
    suffering you have to undo.
  </p>

  <h2>The 140-year dividend</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="life expectancy at birth, England & Wales — approximate series, 1850–2020" widget="plotter" config={lifeExpPlot} />
  <p>
    The curve (approximate values from the Our World in Data / Human Mortality Database
    series) roughly doubles in 170 years: about 40 years in 1850, about 47 by 1900, 68 by
    1950, 77 by 2000, 79 by 2020. Crucially, historians of the "epidemiologic transition"
    attribute most of the early gains not to clinical medicine but to public health —
    sanitation, nutrition, safer births, vaccination. That is the profession's core claim,
    stated in data: the organised efforts of society, measured at the population level,
    added decades to human life.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> CEPH Accreditation Criteria for Schools of Public Health & Public Health Programs (2024), Foundational Knowledge D1; Institute of Medicine, The Future of Public Health (1988); Winslow, "The Untilled Fields of Public Health", Science (1920); WHO records; Our World in Data / Human Mortality Database life-expectancy series.</p>
</TopicPage>
